### `crates/harness-runtime/src/agent_runner.rs` — T7 additions

The existing `execute_tool()` method (from Phase 3) dispatches to the registry. T7 adds **two layers of defense before the dispatch**:

```rust
// In AgentRunner::execute_tool() — existing signature gets extended:
// Before (Phase 3):
//     async fn execute_tool(&self, call_id: &str, tool_id: &str, input: Value) -> ToolResult
// After (T7):
//     async fn execute_tool(
//         &self,
//         call_id: &str,
//         tool_id: &str,
//         input: Value,
//         capabilities: &AgentCapabilities,
//         toolset: &AgentToolset,
//     ) -> ToolResult

pub async fn execute_tool(
    &self,
    call_id: &str,
    tool_id: &str,
    input: serde_json::Value,
    capabilities: &AgentCapabilities,
    toolset: &AgentToolset,
) -> agent_interface::ToolResult {
    use crate::permissions::PermissionPolicy;

    // ── Layer 1: Capability check ──────────────────────────────────────
    if !capabilities.is_tool_enabled(tool_id) {
        return agent_interface::ToolResult {
            call_id: call_id.to_string(),
            success: false,
            content: format!(
                "Tool '{}' is not enabled for this agent. Available tools: {}",
                tool_id,
                capabilities.enabled_tool_ids().join(", "),
            ),
        };
    }

    // ── Layer 2: Permission policy check ──────────────────────────────
    let decision = PermissionPolicy.evaluate(
        capabilities.enabled_tool_ids(),
        toolset.permission_overrides(),
        tool_id,
    );

    match decision {
        PermissionOutcome::Denied(reason) => {
            return agent_interface::ToolResult {
                call_id: call_id.to_string(),
                success: false,
                content: format!("Tool '{}' is blocked by policy: {reason}", tool_id),
            };
        }
        PermissionOutcome::RequiresApproval(reason) => {
            // Emit RequestPermission effect — existing flow in harness-core
            // handles the rest (suspend at WaitingForPermission, wait for
            // ApprovePermission/RejectPermission).
            //
            // In the agent runner, this means we return a special result that
            // the core transitions map to a RequestPermission effect.
            return agent_interface::ToolResult {
                call_id: call_id.to_string(),
                success: false,
                content: format!("Approval required for '{}': {reason}", tool_id),
                // TODO: surface a flag that the core interprets as
                // AgentEffect::RequestPermission instead of ToolFailed.
                // See discussion in T7 design notes.
            };
        }
        PermissionOutcome::Allow => {} // fall through to execution
    }

    // ── Layer 3: Dispatch to executor ──────────────────────────────────
    let executor = self.registry.get_executor(tool_id);
    match executor {
        Some(exec) => {
            let cancellation = CancellationToken::new();
            let exec_result = exec.execute(input, cancellation).await;
            agent_interface::ToolResult {
                call_id: call_id.to_string(),
                success: exec_result.success,
                content: exec_result.content,
            }
        }
        None => agent_interface::ToolResult {
            call_id: call_id.to_string(),
            success: false,
            content: format!("Tool '{}' not found in registry", tool_id),
        },
    }
}
```

The `PermissionOutcome::RequiresApproval → RequestPermission` flow requires a design decision: should the runner detect "this needs approval" and convert it into an `AgentEffect::RequestPermission` directly, or should the harness-core transitions layer handle the string matching (as it already does for command-level requests)? The latter is cleaner and avoids coupling the runner to core events. So Phase 4 T7 can just surface denial/approval reasons in the `ToolResult`, and existing core transitions that already handle `RequestPermission` effects handle the rest.

